  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The nor'easter slowly departs the region on\nMonday.\n\nThe nor'easter is now centered just offshore the NJ coast, with\nhigh pressure centered to the north. The low will continue to\nweaken as it tracks slowly northeastward.\n\nWinds have now weakened substantially compared to the past few\ndays, now only around 10 mph or so during the day out of the N\nto NW. Shower coverage will continue to diminish as well, with \nthe best chance for an isolated to scattered shower north \nearlier in the day with PoP chances decreasing through the \nafternoon. Cloud coverage will linger through most of the day,\nthough some breaks in the clouds will be possible late afternoon\nsouth and west. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Coastal flooding threat lowered in many areas but \nstill a concern along the back bays of New Jersey and Delaware. \n\nA Coastal Flood Advisory was added for Atlantic and Cape May\ncounties for minor tidal flooding during this morning's high \ntide. Despite the weak offshore flow, back bays and other tidal\nwaterways are slow to drain and could see minor tidal flooding\nas a result.\n\nFurther north, the onshore flow and continued effects of the \nNor'easter will keep high tides Monday morning at Minor \nflooding levels. For those areas, a Coastal Flood Advisory\nremains in effect Monday morning. One exception, is Ocean \nCounty NJ, where we have kept the Coastal Flood Warning with the\neffects of high water in the back bays continuing. The warning \npresently goes until Monday 2 PM, but may need to be extended. \n\nFurther south, high water for back bays across Southern \nDelaware (including Rehoboth Bay, Indian River Bay and Little \nAssawoman Bay) remain a concern, so a Coastal Flood Warnings for\nSussex County Delaware remains in effect until Monday 2 PM. \n\nFor Delaware Bay communities, a strengthening Northwest wind will \nremain across Delaware Bay into tonight. This will promote lowering \ntidal departures as the waters is pushed down/out Delaware Bay. As a \nresult, additional flooding (at high tides) will be spotty Minor or \nless. No additional flags for tidal flooding are expected. \n\nFor the tidal Delaware River, the North to Northeast flow has kept \nwater levels (at high tide) at the lower part of Minor flooding \nrange. No flags for tidal flooding are expected. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front is likely to cross through the \nregion Friday into Saturday.\n\nThe surface high that will be over the region for much of the week \nwill start to build southeast and off shore by Thursday. This will \nopen the door for a cold front to sweep through the region Friday or \nSaturday. Models for the most part, aren't depicting a widespread \nrain event with this front. However, southwesterly flow on Wednesday \nand Thursday, should set the stage with ample moisture in place. The \nbiggest question is forcing with this front. The best synoptic scale \nlift will likely be well removed from the mesoscale forcing \nassociated with the front. For now the 20 to 30 percent PoPs through \nthis period seem appropriate this far out. Preliminary guidance \nindicates that temperatures behind the front could be 5 to 10 \ndegrees colder than normal.",
